═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
PART 3: ANALYSIS
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

3.1 WHAT WORKED
──────────────────────────────────────
✓ Forest creation — successfully created population of trees
✓ Fitness evaluation — correctly calculated fitness scores
✓ Selection — selected parents based on fitness
✓ Crossover — combined trees to create offspring
✓ Mutation — applied random modifications
✓ Evolution — improved max fitness over generations

3.2 ISSUES
──────────────────────────────────────
✗ Average fitness decreased — indicates selection pressure too high
✗ Convergence too quick — got stuck in local optimum
✗ Mutation rate too low — not enough exploration
✗ Fitness function too simple — needs more factors

3.3 LESSONS LEARNED
──────────────────────────────────────
1. Selection pressure matters — too high causes premature convergence
2. Mutation rate is critical — too low limits exploration
3. Fitness function complexity — needs multiple factors
4. Elitism helps — preserves best solutions
5. Forest concept works — population-based evolution is viable

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
